so we have some pretty big geopolitics  to talk about today over the weekend  some Saudi oil facilities were attacked  and the Trump administration is  increasingly looking at Iran as the  culprit and before we get into sort of  like the specific analysis anytime in  the Trump era that we're doing an  analysis of geopolitics there's sort of  like the normal analysis one would do  based on the last 200 years of American  history and then you have to modify that  analysis to account for the fact that  Donald Trump is President of the United  States an analogy that came to me over  the weekend is sort of like if you have  an airline pilot that flies a particular  airplane right an Airbus 330 and you say  what is the take-off speed of the Airbus  330 and you have you're like in a vacuum  answer which might be a hundred and  fifty knots but then much like you have  to account for Donald Trump being  president in geopolitics you then have  to account for whether the runway is dry  or wet and what is the elevation of the  airport and other things and you might  end up with an answer where well in this  particular case the take-off speed for  an Airbus 330 is 160 knots when  accounting for these other factors and  similarly with Donald Trump thrown into  geopolitics we have to account for  Donald Trump in making our evaluation so  a few different things to go over it's  the event that took place the hypocrisy  from Donald Trump and also the growing  subservience by Donald Trump to the  Saudis which is something that we should  all be extremely worried about in  particular because of the connections we  already know Trump and Jared Kushner and  others around him have to the Saudis so  first of all why the focus on Iran so  far what we know is that a number of  Saudi oil facilities were attacked over  the weekend we have satellite images  that show 17 points of impact coming  from the north or the northwest which  could be Iran it could be Iraq  now in the immediate and the Iranian  backed Houthi militias are hootie  militias and never sure how to pronounce  that from Yemen claimed responsibility  and there are reasons to be skeptical  about this the direction of the attacks  is at least a potential reason to be  skeptical the use of cruise missiles  and drones seem to exceed the known  capabilities of the Hootie's and Donald  Trump as usual is reacting to everything  in the worst possible way  erratic indirect and sort of showing his  cards that he is indeed subservient to  the Saudis hypocritical subservience is  maybe how I would sum it up so first of  all Donald Trump tweeting Saudi Arabia  oil supply was attacked there is reason  to believe that we know the culprit are  locked and loaded depending on  verification but are waiting to hear  from the kingdom as to who they believe  was the cause of this attack and under  what terms we would proceed look at all  of the extremely disturbing elements  just to this one tweet hinting at Iran  but not saying it we have reason to  believe we know who did it why not just  come out and say it  locked and loaded it's a threat of  violence but again it's predicated on  Donald Trump having reason to believe  that he knows who it is but saying that  once the Saudis tell him who they  believe did it he's locked and loaded  and ready to go under whatever terms the  Saudis say are the ones that Donald  Trump should operate under this is a  level of subservience to the Saudis and  then we get to the hypocrisy because  Donald Trump in 2014  tweeted quote Saudi Arabia should fight  their own Wars which they won't or pay  us an absolute fortune to protect them  and their great wealth trillion dollars  of course Donald Trump was not present  at the time Barack Obama was president  at the time  so was that just BS and political  bluster probably the other possibility  is Donald Trump actually has made some  kind of deal with the Saudis for the  u.s. to be their protector and in a way  that deal has sort of been made in plain  sight for decades through American  foreign policy Donald Trump may well see  and it actually wouldn't be that crazy  he may see the endless and infinitely  growing arms deals with Saudi Arabia as  that sort of payment to the United  States for the protection  of the Saudis you may recall that when  Barack Obama sort of semi bowed to the  Saudis when they were greeting each  other the right went crazy even though  George W Bush was kissing the Saudis on  the cheek and they seemed fine with it  at the time now Donald Trump is saying  when the Saudis tell me who they think  did it and when the Saudis tell me what  the appropriate response would be we're  gonna go out and do it really strong  leader right projecting strength and  independence and focusing on what's best  for the oh wait no he's really not some  of the bigger political questions here  does Donald Trump want a war with  someone to help the arms industry is  that sort of what is at the core of  what's going on here does Donald Trump  want a war with someone so that he can  finally be the tough guy that he of  course is not but desperately wants to  be does Donald Trump want a war with  someone because he believes it will help  his reelection after all that would not  be incorrect in a sense I'm trying to  think of when the United States voted  out an incumbent president in the middle  of a war or significant military  engagement and I'm having trouble  thinking of that maybe there are  examples and or because this is all  obviously not just one thing it could be  a combination diss Donald Trump want a  war because it will be a distraction  from one of his many current or upcoming  scandals a combination is usually sort  of the right answer with this president  I got a lot of emails from the audience  over the last year saying David at some  point Trump is going to war the question  is who will it be against and under what  pretenses but the guy is going to find a  war one way or another either John  Bolton is going to help him or if John  Bolton is the impediment Bolton will go  which he since has and Trump will find a  war on his own maybe not more cynical  than reality actually suggests here and  of course immediately the economic  impact is significant relatively minor  in the context of the broader insanity  but oil price is spiking about one sixth  from sixty to seventy one dollars we  don't even get that much of our oil from  Saudi Arabia the notion that we are  dependent on the Saudis for the oil  itself is wrong I mean they have a big  market share  but from my recollection we get around  11% of our total oil consumption from  foreign countries and of that 11%  I think it's 9% only is from Saudi  Arabia so the actual numbers don't bear  it out but of course a massive player in  OPEC and in controlling the oil market  so an economic impact right away I don't  know where this will go we'll keep  following it I want to hear from you  about it but this is of course going to  be a very very crazy year leading up to  the November 20 2011
 

